//! The Time module: monotonic and wall-clock time, and durations that arithmetic works on.
//!
//! Written in Jairs. PLAN §8.3 put this first in W7's remaining order for one reason: it is the cheapest
//! real module left — `clock_gettime` is scalars and a pointer, which is the FFI shape that already works
//! (ADR-0150 refuses aggregates at that boundary, and nothing here needs one) — and it gives every later
//! benchmark a clock.
//!
//! # Why nanoseconds, and why an `s64`
//!
//! One integer unit throughout, so a duration is a *number* and arithmetic on it needs no library. An
//! `s64` of nanoseconds spans about 292 years either side of the epoch, which outlives any program this
//! language will compile, and it is **exact** — a `float64` of seconds loses nanosecond resolution
//! somewhere in the 2030s and would make two runs of the same benchmark differ in their last digits for
//! no reason a reader could see.
//!
//! Rejected: a `Duration` struct with a seconds/nanos pair, which is what C's `timespec` is. It cannot be
//! subtracted without a helper, cannot be compared without another, and its only advantage — range —
//! is one this unit does not need. Rejected too: microseconds or milliseconds as the base unit, because a
//! coarser unit cannot express what the finer one measures and every timing question here is sub-millisecond.
//!
//! # Why two clocks, and why the difference matters
//!
//! `monotonic()` never goes backwards and is the only correct thing to *measure* with: a wall clock can
//! jump when the system clock is set or when NTP steps it, so a duration computed from two wall-clock
//! readings can be negative or wildly wrong. `wall()` is what a *timestamp* wants — it is comparable
//! between machines and survives a reboot.
//!
//! Both are offered because using the wrong one is a real and quiet bug: a benchmark on `wall()` reports a
//! nonsense number occasionally rather than always, which is the hardest kind to notice.
//!
//! # What is absent, and why
//!
//! No formatting — no `to_string`, no strftime. Rendering a timestamp needs a calendar, and a calendar
//! needs leap seconds, time zones and a locale, none of which this project has decided anything about.
//! A module that offered a *wrong* rendering would be worse than one that offers none, and PLAN §2.1's
//! `Time` entry is about measurement.
//!
//! No sleeping. `nanosleep` is a syscall that blocks, and a blocking call in the comptime VM would mean
//! compilation that pauses — which is a decision about compile-time execution rather than about time
//! (ADR-0121 gave comptime a step budget precisely so it cannot run away).

#import "Basic";

/// `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`, selected for the target (ADR-0181 §1).
///
/// **This constant is the reason `os()` exists.** It was `6` — macOS's number — with a comment saying so
/// and adding *"the day the CI matrix runs is the day this needs a `#if`-shaped answer, which this
/// language does not have either"*. It has one now, and it is not `#if`: `os()` is a compile-time **value**
/// (ADR-0180), so a per-OS number is selected by an ordinary procedure that a `#run` evaluates — the
/// file-scope idiom `modules/Random`'s `GOLDEN :: #run golden_seed();` already used.
///
/// Windows gets **0**, which is not a guess: Windows has no `clock_gettime` at all, so there is no correct
/// number, and `0` is `CLOCK_REALTIME`'s value — the reading a caller would get if the binding somehow
/// resolved, and otherwise the failure `clock_gettime`'s `-1` already reports. Naming a plausible-looking
/// constant for a call that cannot happen would be the worse answer.
monotonic_clock_id :: () -> s64 {
    if os() == Operating_System.MACOS {
        return 6;
    }
    if os() == Operating_System.LINUX {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

CLOCK_MONOTONIC :: #run monotonic_clock_id();

/// `CLOCK_REALTIME`, which is 0 on both macOS and Linux — so this constant, unlike the one above, is
/// genuinely portable.
CLOCK_REALTIME :: 0;

/// A `timespec` as C lays it out: two 64-bit words on both supported targets.
///
/// Declared here rather than passed as two `*s64`s because `clock_gettime` takes **one** pointer to this
/// struct, and a pointer to a Jairs struct is a pointer — which crosses the FFI boundary fine. It is the
/// *aggregate by value* that does not (ADR-0150), and nothing here passes one.
Timespec :: struct {
    /// Whole seconds.
    seconds: s64;
    /// Nanoseconds within the second, 0 to 999_999_999.
    nanoseconds: s64;
}

/// POSIX `clock_gettime(2)`: writes the named clock's reading into `out`.
///
/// Returns 0 on success and -1 on failure. It is **not** `#must`, deliberately: the only documented
/// failures are an invalid clock id and an unwritable pointer, both of which are programming errors this
/// module's own callers cannot make — `now` passes a constant id and the address of a local. A `#must`
/// here would push a check onto every caller for a case that cannot arise, and ADR-0151 §2's point is that
/// the marker means something because it is not everywhere.
clock_gettime :: (clock: s64, out: *Timespec) -> s64 #foreign libc "clock_gettime";

/// Nanoseconds since an unspecified origin, from a clock that never goes backwards.
///
/// The only correct thing to measure a duration with. Two readings subtract to a nanosecond count.
monotonic :: () -> s64 {
    return now(CLOCK_MONOTONIC);
}

/// Nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, from the wall clock.
///
/// Comparable between machines and across a reboot, and therefore what a *timestamp* wants — but it can
/// jump, so a duration computed from two of these can be negative. Use `monotonic` to measure.
wall :: () -> s64 {
    return now(CLOCK_REALTIME);
}

/// One clock's reading, as nanoseconds.
///
/// Shared by the two above so the seconds-to-nanoseconds conversion exists once: getting it wrong in one
/// of two copies would make one clock a thousand times faster than the other, which is exactly the kind of
/// silent wrong answer a duplicated computation produces.
///
/// `0` when the clock cannot be read, which is unreachable for the two ids above — see `clock_gettime`.
now :: (clock: s64) -> s64 {
    t: Timespec;
    if clock_gettime(clock, *t) != 0 {
        return 0;
    }
    return t.seconds * NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND + t.nanoseconds;
}

/// Nanoseconds in a second.
NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND :: 1000000000;

/// Nanoseconds in a millisecond.
NANOSECONDS_PER_MILLISECOND :: 1000000;

/// Nanoseconds in a microsecond.
NANOSECONDS_PER_MICROSECOND :: 1000;

/// `nanoseconds` as whole milliseconds, truncating.
///
/// Truncating rather than rounding, and stated because the choice is invisible at the call site: a reader
/// comparing `to_milliseconds(x) == 5` wants to know whether 5_999_999 counts. It does not.
to_milliseconds :: (nanoseconds: s64) -> s64 {
    return nanoseconds / NANOSECONDS_PER_MILLISECOND;
}

/// `nanoseconds` as whole microseconds, truncating.
to_microseconds :: (nanoseconds: s64) -> s64 {
    return nanoseconds / NANOSECONDS_PER_MICROSECOND;
}

/// `nanoseconds` as whole seconds, truncating.
to_seconds :: (nanoseconds: s64) -> s64 {
    return nanoseconds / NANOSECONDS_PER_SECOND;
}

/// Whether `a` is a later reading than `b` on the same clock.
///
/// A named comparison rather than leaving callers to write `a > b`, because on the *wall* clock the
/// comparison is the thing that can surprise: a reading taken later can be numerically smaller. This does
/// not fix that — nothing can — but a caller who reaches for a helper is a caller who has thought about it.
is_after :: (a: s64, b: s64) -> bool {
    return a > b;
}
